.TH NA_HAMLIB_BRIDGE 1 "2026-10-22" "naudio" "naudio manual"
.SH NAME
na_hamlib_bridge \- bridge a Hamlib rig_stream audio stream onto the naudio wire
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B na_hamlib_bridge
.RB [ \-m
.IR model ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR naudio_port ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR channels ]
.RB [ \-R
.IR wan | lan | ft8 | tcp | dual ]
.RB [ \-S
.IR stream_mode ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR token = value ]\ ...
.RB [ \-k ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B na_hamlib_bridge
re-originates a Hamlib
.BR rig_stream_ *
audio stream onto naudio's FEC-native wire, so rig audio survives a lossy
LAN/WAN hop.
It runs at the radio site, upstream of that hop: Hamlib talks to the rig
(directly, or via
.BR rigctld (1)
over netrigctl), and the bridge serves the rig's RX audio as a naudio
stream while feeding client TX audio back to the rig.
.PP
The streaming subsystem it is built on is Hamlib PR #2116 (Hamlib 5.0+),
which no released Hamlib ships yet \(em the bridge must be linked against a
streaming-capable libhamlib.
In upstream Hamlib master the Dummy is still the only backend with
.IR stream_caps .
Hamlib PR #2178 (open) adds Icom network backends (models 3095\(en3100)
that implement them, and draft PR #2213 extends the FlexRadio backend with
audio and I/Q streaming; naudio has not run the bridge against a radio, so
real-rig behaviour is unverified.
The Dummy backend (model 1) exercises the full path hardware-free.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " model"
Hamlib model number (default 1 = DUMMY, hardware-free; 2 = NETRIGCTL).
.TP
.BI \-r " address"
the rig's address, set as Hamlib's
.I rig_pathname
token: host:port of a
.BR rigctld (1)
for model 2, e.g. localhost:5555, or the radio's own address for a
network radio.
.TP
.BI \-S " stream_mode"
backend stream_mode configuration.
The Dummy backend accepts
.IR tone ,
.IR silence ,
.IR loopback ,
.IR counter ;
.I loopback
feeds TX back out of RX \(em a hardware-free TX round trip.
.TP
.BI \-C " token\fR=\fPvalue"
set a Hamlib configuration token before the rig is opened.
Repeatable; applied in the order given, after
.B \-r
and
.BR \-S ,
so
.B \-C stream_mode=tone
does what
.B \-S tone
does and overrides it.
An Icom network radio logs in with two of these,
.I net_username
and
.IR net_password .
.B rigctl \-m
.I model
.B \-L
lists a model's tokens.
A token the model does not have, a malformed argument, or a value the
backend refuses stops the bridge with a message naming the token (a
malformed argument only up to the end of its leading name).
The bridge never prints the value; libhamlib's own diagnostics may, on the
same standard error (see the guide named below).
A value libhamlib refuses for
.B \-r
or
.B \-S
only draws a warning, while a refused
.B \-C
stops the bridge.
Command-line arguments are visible to other users of the machine (for
example through
.BR ps (1)),
so a password given here is too, as is one in a service definition that
carries the arguments, such as a systemd unit or a launchd plist.
.TP
.BI \-p " naudio_port"
port the bridge's naudio server listens on (default 4533).
.TP
.BI \-c " channels"
1 or 2 (default 1).
Stereo over netrigctl needs a libhamlib at or after Hamlib commit
961093f2 on
.B this
side; the bridge prints the applicable advice when it detects RX gaps.
.TP
.BI \-R " wan\fR|\fPlan\fR|\fPft8\fR|\fPtcp\fR|\fPdual"
naudio transport and reliability profile.
.IR wan ,
the default, is UDP with FEC, for a lossy hop;
.I lan
and
.I ft8
are UDP without FEC.
.I tcp
serves plain TCP, and
.I dual
serves TCP and UDP on the same port.
A client that selects no profile connects over TCP \(em the network-audio
input WSJT-X has in development is one \(em and the UDP profiles refuse it,
so serve it with
.I tcp
or
.IR dual .
Any other name is a usage error.
.TP
.B \-k
key the rig's PTT around client TX bursts.
.TP
.B \-x
disable the TX (operator-to-rig) direction; RX only.
.TP
.B \-h
print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
clean run.
.TP
.B 1
startup or streaming failure, including a
.B \-C
token the model does not have or a value it refuses.
.TP
.B 2
usage error, including an unknown
.B \-R
profile and a malformed
.B \-C
argument.
.SH EXAMPLES
Hardware-free end to end, serving a test tone from the Dummy rig:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_hamlib_bridge \-m 1 \-S tone
.RE
.fi
.PP
Bridge a remote rigctld, RX only, FT8-tuned reliability:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_hamlib_bridge \-m 2 \-r radio\-host:5555 \-x \-R ft8
.RE
.fi
.PP
Serve a TCP-only client, such as the network-audio input WSJT-X has in
development, on the same machine or LAN:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_hamlib_bridge \-m 1 \-S tone \-R tcp
.RE
.fi
.PP
An Icom IC-7610 on the LAN, logging in as the network user configured in
the radio's menu (needs a libhamlib with Hamlib PR #2178's Icom network
backends; naudio has not run this against a radio):
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_hamlib_bridge \-m 3095 \-r 192.168.1.50 \e
    \-C net_username=USER \-C net_password=PASS
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR na_audio_daemon (1),
.BR na_wav_tap (1),
.BR rigctld (1)
.PP
Building the streaming libhamlib this tool needs, and why a skipped build
looks like a broken flag:
.I docs/hamlib\-streaming\-bridge.md
in the naudio source tree
(https://github.com/KJ5HST\-LABS/naudio).
